hashmap
=======

Version 0.0.1 (September, 2026)

A general-purpose hash table library for C.

Hashmap is a small C library implementing a dynamically sized hash table. It
stores arbitrary binary keys and arbitrary binary data and manages its own
copies of both.

The library is designed to be simple to use while providing predictable behavior
when the table grows or shrinks. Resizing is performed incrementally during
normal operation, so ordinary insertions and deletions do not require moving the
entire table at once.

The implementation uses separate chaining: the table consists of an array of
buckets, and each bucket contains a linked list of entries.

The library can be used directly by compiling hashmap.c into an application, or
as a shared library that can be installed and linked against by other programs.

General Characteristics
-----------------------

- Written in C.
- General-purpose key/value storage.
- Keys may contain arbitrary binary data.
- Values may contain arbitrary binary data.
- Key and value sizes are explicitly specified.
- The hashmap makes private copies of keys and values.
- The hashmap owns all stored keys and values.
- Separate chaining is used to resolve hash collisions.
- The number of buckets grows and shrinks dynamically.
- Bucket counts are powers of two.
- Normal resizing is incremental.
- Two entries are migrated per insertion or deletion while a resize is
  in progress.
- Insertions and searches work while a resize is in progress.
- Entries may be found in either the current or old table during a
  migration.
- Configuration parameters can be changed at runtime.
- Configuration changes can cause an immediate, complete migration.
- A configuration change can safely occur while an incremental migration
  is already in progress.
- The library can automatically create a hashmap when inserting into a
  NULL hashmap pointer.
- The entire hashmap can be destroyed with hashmap_clear().
- The public API does not expose the internal bucket or entry structures.

Keys and Values
---------------

Both keys and values are represented by a pointer and a size.

For example:

int key = 123;
int value = 456;

hash_insert(&map,
            &key, sizeof(key),
            &value, sizeof(value));

The hashmap copies both objects. The caller may therefore modify or release the
original objects after the operation returns.

The hashmap does not store pointers to caller-owned key or value objects.

Binary data is supported. A key or value may contain zero bytes and does not
have to be a C string.

A size of zero is valid. When the size is zero, the corresponding pointer may be
NULL.

For a non-zero key size, the key pointer must not be NULL.

For a non-zero data size, the data pointer must not be NULL.

Hash Table Organization
-----------------------

The table contains a number of buckets. Each bucket is the head of a linked list
of entries.

Conceptually, the table looks like this:

+---------+
| bucket 0|----> entry ----> entry ----> NULL
+---------+
| bucket 1|----> NULL
+---------+
| bucket 2|----> entry ----> NULL
+---------+
|   ...   |
+---------+
| bucket N|----> entry ----> entry ----> NULL
+---------+

Each entry contains:

key
key size
data
data size
pointer to next entry

The current table and an old table can temporarily coexist during an incremental
resize.

Resizing
--------

The hashmap automatically resizes according to its load thresholds.

The default configuration is:

grow threshold   = 1.2
shrink threshold = 0.3
resize factor    = 2
minimum buckets  = 16

The resize factor is currently fixed by resize_shift = 1, which means that a
resize doubles or halves the number of buckets.

Normal resizing is incremental.

When a resize begins, the current table becomes the old table and a new table
becomes the current table. New entries are inserted into the new table.

Existing entries are gradually moved from the old table to the new table.
Two entries are migrated after each insertion or deletion.

During this period:

- searches examine both tables;
- deletion can remove an entry from either table;
- new insertions go into the current table;
- the total number of logical entries is the sum of the entries in
  both tables.

The migration continues until the old table is empty.

This approach avoids requiring a normal insertion or deletion to migrate the
entire hashmap at once.

RUNTIME CONFIGURATION

The following parameters can currently be changed with hashmap_opt():

HASHMAP_GROW_THRESHOLD
HASHMAP_SHRINK_THRESHOLD
HASHMAP_MIN_BUCKETS

The resize factor is currently fixed at two.

Before a new configuration is applied, the configuration is validated.

A configuration change may require a change in the number of buckets.

The required number of buckets is determined iteratively.

Starting with the current number of buckets:

1. If the table requires growth under the new configuration, double the number
   of buckets and check again.

2. Once growth is no longer required, check whether shrinking is required.

3. If shrinking is required and the minimum bucket limit permits it, halve the
   number of buckets and check again.

4. Stop when neither growth nor further shrinking is required.

If a configuration change does not require changing the number of buckets, an
existing incremental migration is completed immediately if one is in progress.

If the required number of buckets differs from the current number, a new bucket
array is created and the entries are migrated into it immediately.

If an incremental migration was already in progress, entries from both the
current and old tables are migrated into the newly created bucket array.

Thus, a successful configuration change leaves the hashmap in a stable state
with no migration in progress.

Memory ownership
----------------

The hashmap owns every key and data object stored in it.

Memory is allocated internally when an entry is inserted.

When an entry is deleted, its key and data are freed automatically.

When the entire hashmap is cleared, all entries, their keys and data, the bucket
table, and the hashmap object itself are released.

The caller must not free pointers returned through hash_search().

The data pointer returned by hash_search() is borrowed from the hashmap. It
remains valid until the corresponding entry is deleted or its data is replaced
by hash_update() or hash_put().

API
---

hashmap_t *hashmap_create(void);

Creates and returns a new empty hashmap.

The initial number of buckets is determined by the default minimum bucket
configuration.

Return value:

Non-NULL hashmap pointer on success.
NULL if memory allocation fails.

void hashmap_clear(hashmap_t **map);

Destroys the hashmap and all entries stored in it.

The function accepts a pointer to the hashmap pointer. On return, the caller
pointer is set to NULL.

Example:

hashmap_clear(&map);

After the call:

map == NULL

int hash_probe(const hashmap_t *map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size);

Checks whether a key exists in the hashmap.

The function does not return the associated data.

The function searches both the current and old table when a migration is in
progress.

Arguments:

map
    Hashmap to search. May be NULL.

key
    Pointer to the key. May be NULL when key_size is zero.

key_size
    Size of the key in bytes.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    The key exists.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    The key does not exist.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

int hash_search(hashmap_t *map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size,
const void **data,
size_t *data_size);

Searches for a key and returns a pointer to its associated data.

The returned data belongs to the hashmap and must not be freed or modified by
the caller.

Arguments:

map
    Hashmap to search.

key
    Pointer to the key. May be NULL when key_size is zero.

key_size
    Size of the key in bytes.

data
    Address of a pointer where the hashmap's data pointer is returned.

data_size
    Address of a size_t where the data size is returned.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    The key exists and its data was returned.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    The key does not exist.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

For an entry whose data size is zero:

*data == NULL
*data_size == 0

int hash_insert(hashmap_t **map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size,
const void *data,
size_t data_size);

Inserts a new key/data pair.

The hashmap makes copies of both the key and the data.

If *map is NULL, an empty hashmap is automatically created.

If the key already exists, the insertion fails and the existing entry is not
replaced.

Arguments:

map
    Address of the hashmap pointer.

key
    Pointer to the key. May be NULL when key_size is zero.

key_size
    Size of the key in bytes.

data
    Pointer to the data. May be NULL when data_size is zero.

data_size
    Size of the data in bytes.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Entry inserted successfully.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    Operation failed, for example because of memory allocation failure or
    because the key already exists.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

int hash_update(hashmap_t *map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size,
const void *data,
size_t data_size);

Replaces the data associated with an existing key.

The key itself is not replaced.

The new data is allocated and copied before the old data is released. If
allocation fails, the existing data remains unchanged.

Arguments:

map
    Hashmap to modify.

key
    Pointer to the key. May be NULL when key_size is zero.

key_size
    Size of the key in bytes.

data
    Pointer to the new data. May be NULL when data_size is zero.

data_size
    Size of the new data in bytes.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Data updated successfully.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    The key does not exist or the operation failed.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

Updating an entry does not change the number of entries in the hashmap.

int hash_put(hashmap_t **map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size,
const void *data,
size_t data_size);

Inserts a new entry or updates an existing entry.

If the key does not exist, hash_put() behaves like hash_insert().

If the key already exists, its data is replaced as with hash_update().

If *map is NULL, an empty hashmap is automatically created.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Entry inserted or updated successfully.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    Operation failed.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

hash_put() is useful when the caller does not need to distinguish between an
insertion and an update.

int hash_delete(hashmap_t **map,
const void *key,
size_t key_size);

Deletes the entry associated with a key.

The key and data owned by the hashmap are released.

If the deleted entry is the last entry in the hashmap, the entire hashmap is
destroyed and *map is set to NULL.

The function searches both tables when a migration is in progress.

Arguments:

map
    Address of the hashmap pointer.

key
    Pointer to the key. May be NULL when key_size is zero.

key_size
    Size of the key in bytes.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Entry deleted successfully.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    The key does not exist.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

Because deletion of the last entry destroys the hashmap, callers should not use
the hashmap after hash_delete() without first checking whether the pointer is
NULL.

size_t hashmap_getsize(hashmap_t *map);

Returns the number of logical entries currently stored in the hashmap.

During an incremental resize, entries may temporarily exist in both the current and old tables. They
are nevertheless counted only once.

Conceptually:

logical entries =
    current-table entries + old-table entries

Return value:

Number of logical entries.

For a NULL hashmap:

0

int hashmap_info(const hashmap_t *map,
hashmap_info_t *info);

Returns information about the current runtime state of the hashmap.

The structure is:

typedef struct {
    size_t nr_buckets;
    size_t nr_entries;
    float load_factor;
    bool resize_in_progress;
    size_t nr_buckets_old;
    size_t nr_entries_old;
    size_t resize_bucket;
} hashmap_info_t;

Fields:

nr_buckets
    Number of buckets in the current table.

nr_entries
    Number of entries currently stored in the current table.

load_factor
    Load factor of the current table.

resize_in_progress
    True when an incremental migration is in progress.

nr_buckets_old
    Number of buckets in the old table during migration.

nr_entries_old
    Number of entries remaining in the old table during migration.

resize_bucket
    Current migration position in the old table.

During a migration, the total number of logical entries is:

nr_entries + nr_entries_old

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Information returned successfully.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

int hashmap_opt(hashmap_t *map,
const int key,
float value);

Changes one hashmap configuration parameter.

Available keys:

HASHMAP_GROW_THRESHOLD
HASHMAP_SHRINK_THRESHOLD
HASHMAP_MIN_BUCKETS

The value is interpreted according to the selected option.

For HASHMAP_GROW_THRESHOLD:

value is the new growth threshold.

For HASHMAP_SHRINK_THRESHOLD:

value is the new shrink threshold.

For HASHMAP_MIN_BUCKETS:

value is converted to the corresponding bucket-count value.

The configuration must satisfy the hashmap's configuration constraints.

A successful option change may trigger an immediate migration.

Return value:

HASHMAP_OK
    Configuration changed successfully.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid configuration, invalid option, or another error.

RETURN VALUES

The public API currently uses the following return values:

HASHMAP_OK
    Success.

HASHMAP_ERROR
    General operation failure or requested object not found, depending on the
    function.

HASHMAP_INVALID_ARG
    Invalid argument.

Some functions may have more specific internal error distinctions, but the
public interface currently exposes the values above.

Compilation
-----------

The library can be compiled directly with the supplied Makefile.

From the project directory:

make

This produces:

libhashmap.so

The supplied build uses:

-std=c11
-Wall
-Wextra
-Wpedantic
-Werror
-fPIC

The library is built as a shared object.

Running the Tests
-----------------

The project contains a separate test suite.

Run:

make test

The test build automatically discovers the C source files in the tests
directory.

The tests are linked against libhashmap.so.

To remove generated objects, the shared library, and test executables:

make clean

Using the Library Without Installing It
---------------------------------------

A program can include the public header:

#include <hashmap.h>

and link against the locally built shared library.

For example:

cc -I/path/to/hashmap \
   -L/path/to/hashmap \
   -o my_program my_program.c \
   -lhashmap

When running the program, the dynamic linker must be able to locate
libhashmap.so.

For a library located in the project directory, one simple development setup is:

LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/path/to/hashmap ./my_program

Installation
------------

The current Makefile builds the shared library but does not install it
system-wide.

A system-wide installation can be performed manually by placing:

hashmap.h

in an appropriate system include directory and:

libhashmap.so

in an appropriate library directory.

After installation, applications can include:

#include <hashmap.h>

and link with:

-lhashmap

The exact installation directories depend on the operating system and local
system configuration.

Drop-In Use
-----------

The library can also be used without installing it system-wide.

An application can keep hashmap.c and hashmap.h in its own source tree and
compile hashmap.c together with the application.

For example:

cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic \
   -o my_program my_program.c hashmap.c

The application then only needs:

#include <hashmap.h>

This makes the library suitable for small standalone programs as well as larger
projects.

Typical Use Cases
-----------------

Hashmap is intended as a general-purpose associative container.

Typical applications include:

- mapping strings to configuration values;
- mapping identifiers to application objects;
- counting occurrences of arbitrary keys;
- implementing symbol tables;
- caching calculated results;
- maintaining lookup tables;
- associating network or protocol identifiers with state;
- storing binary keys and binary records;
- implementing application-level indexes;
- building other data structures that require fast key lookup.

Because keys and values are represented as arbitrary byte sequences rather than
null-terminated strings, the library is also useful in situations where a
conventional string-keyed dictionary is insufficient.

For example, a program can use a structure, integer, UUID, binary protocol
field, or other fixed-size object as a key without converting it to a string
first.

Current Limitations
-------------------

The current implementation uses its built-in hash function and key comparison
function.

Support for user-supplied hash and comparison functions may be added in the
future.

The resize factor is currently fixed at two.

The current public API provides configuration changes one option at a time,
through hashmap_opt(). A batch configuration interface may be added in the
future if needed.

Thread Safety
-------------

The library does not provide internal synchronization.

A hashmap object should therefore not be accessed concurrently from multiple
threads when at least one thread may modify it, unless the application provides
appropriate external synchronization.

